  "attributionNote": "Seventy-two or seventy-three verses by Isvarakrishna, who names himself in the last verse, written as a summary of the older Samkhya teaching that had been carried in now-lost sutras.",
  "description": "The Samkhya Karika states the classical Samkhya in seventy verses: the twenty-five principles from material nature to consciousness, the three constituents of nature, the evolution of intellect, ego, mind and the senses, the nature of bondage and the recognition by which the conscious principle is released from nature. It is the shortest complete statement of one of India's oldest philosophical systems.",
  "historicalContext": "The Karika displaced earlier Samkhya sutras to become the school's standard text, and its commentary tradition runs from Gaudapada and Mathara through Vachaspati Mishra. Its analysis of consciousness and nature was absorbed into Vedanta, medicine and the yoga tradition.",
